I'll help you create a navigation bar component for GridPass. Here's a clean implementation using Tailwind CSS:


import { Button } from 'lucide-react'

function NavigationBar() {
  const locations = [
    { name: 'New York', color: 'amber-500' },
    { name: 'San Francisco', color: 'amber-500' },
    { name: 'London', color: 'amber-500' },
    { name: 'Tokyo', color: 'amber-500' }
  ];

  return (
    <div className="container mx-auto px-4 py-2">
      <div className="flex justify-between items-center space-x-8">
        <div className="text-white font-bold text-xl">GridPass</div>
        
        {locations.map((location) => (
          <Button 
            key={location.name}
            className={`${location.color} hover:scale-105 transition-all`}:hover
            onClick={() => {
              // Add click handler here
            }}
          >
            <Zap className="w-4 h-4 text-white" />
            {location.name}
          </Button>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}


This implementation includes:
- Responsive design that works on both desktop and mobile
- Hover scaling effect with smooth transition
- Clean grid layout for the navigation items
- Proper spacing using Tailwind's utility classes
- Dynamic content display based on the locations array

You can customize the colors, spacing, and other properties by adjusting the Tailwind classes. The `Zap` icon is used consistently across all buttons while keeping the design simple and clean.

To use this component, import it at the top of your file:


import { NavigationBar } from './components/NavBar'


And then use it in your main component:


function HomePage() {
  return (
    <div className="min-h-screen bg-neutral-900">
      <Navbar className="w-full h-12 bg-neutral-800">
        <NavigationBar />
      </Navbar>
      {/* Your page content */}
    </div>
  )
}